To achieve smooth, continuous playback of Coqui TTS-generated audio streams on iOS (similar to live streaming), here are three recommended technologies with implementation guidance:

1. ​​AVPlayer + HLS (HTTP Live Streaming)​​
​​Best for:​​ Native integration and adaptive streaming.
​​How it works:​​

Coqui TTS generates audio chunks in real-time, which are segmented into small .ts files and served via an HLS playlist (.m3u8).
iOS uses AVPlayer to stream the playlist URL, automatically appending new segments as they arrive.
​​Implementation steps:​​
a. Set up a server to dynamically update the HLS playlist as Coqui TTS generates new audio chunks.
b. Configure AVPlayer with the HLS URL (e.g., AVPlayer(url: hlsStreamURL)).
c. Use AVPlayerViewController for playback controls and buffering management.
​​Pros:​​
Native support for adaptive bitrate streaming.
Minimal latency with chunked transfers.
Automatic buffering and synchronization.
​​Cons:​​
Requires server infrastructure to generate HLS playlists.
2. ​​AudioQueue Services (Low-Level C API)​​
​​Best for:​​ Real-time buffer management and low latency.
​​How it works:​​

Use AudioQueue to enqueue audio buffers as they arrive from Coqui TTS.
Callbacks trigger playback of each buffer while new data is appended.
​​Implementation steps:​​
a. Initialize AudioQueue with a callback function to handle buffer readiness.
b. Convert Coqui TTS output to PCM audio buffers (e.g., via AVAudioConverter).
c. Enqueue buffers using AudioQueueEnqueueBuffer and manage timing to avoid gaps.
​​Pros:​​
Direct control over audio pipeline.
Suitable for live scenarios with variable chunk sizes.
​​Cons:​​
Complex implementation (manual buffer synchronization).
Requires handling interruptions (e.g., phone calls).
3. ​​AudioStreaming Library (Swift/AVAudioEngine)​​
​​Best for:​​ Simplified integration with Swift.
​​How it works:​​

Use the open-source AudioStreaming library, which leverages AVAudioEngine to process audio chunks.
Append TTS-generated audio data to a circular buffer for continuous playback.
​​Implementation steps:​​
a. Initialize AudioPlayer and feed chunks via player.appendAudioData(_:).
b. Configure real-time playback with AVAudioEngine for mixing and scheduling.
c. Handle network streaming using URLSession to fetch TTS chunks incrementally.
​​Pros:​​
Swift-native API with built-in queue management.
Supports gapless playback and dynamic audio formats.
​​Cons:​​
Limited to supported formats (MP3, AAC, WAV).
